Initial Triage (Data Collection)
	• Traffic Origin: External (Internet) 
	• IP Ownership: Pool Address (112.80.0.0 – 112.87.255.255) 
	• ISP/Organization: China Unicom (China169 Backbone) 
	• Web Hosting: No 
	• IP Reputation: Malicious
(Verified via threat intelligence sources such as VirusTotal, AbuseIPDB, Cisco Talos) 
	
Traffic & Payload Analysis
	• The request contains a JavaScript payload embedded in the URL, indicating a potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attempt. 
	• Payload observed: <$script>javascript:$alert(1)</script>
	• Log analysis showed repeated (persistent) requests from the same source IP, suggesting automated or scripted attack behavior. 
	• The server responded with HTTP 302 (Redirection), indicating: 
		○ The request was processed but redirected 
		○ No clear evidence of successful payload execution 
		
Threat Analysis
	• Attack Type: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Attempt 
	• Attack Nature: Unplanned / Real-world malicious traffic 
	• Persistence: Yes (Repeated attempts observed) 
	• Success Status: Unsuccessful 
		○ No indicators of compromise found on the target system 
		○ No abnormal behavior detected on the endpoint 
		
Investigation Actions
	1. Analyzed HTTP request fields (URL, headers, payload). 
	2. Reviewed logs in the Log Management/SIEM platform for repeated patterns. 
	3. Investigated source IP reputation using threat intelligence tools. 
	4. Checked endpoint and server behavior for signs of compromise. 
	5. Verified that the activity was not part of an internal security test. 

Escalation Decision
	• Tier 2 Escalation Required: No 
Reason:
	• Attack originated from the internet 
	• No evidence of successful exploitation 
	• No internal system compromise detected 

Conclusion
A malicious external IP attempted a Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attack by injecting JavaScript code into a URL parameter. Although the request was allowed and resulted in a redirection (HTTP 302), there is no evidence that the attack was successful.
This activity is classified as a failed web attack attempt and does not require further escalation.

 Mitigation
	• Implement or strengthen Web Application Firewall (WAF) rules to block XSS patterns. 
	• Apply input validation and output encoding on web applications. 
	• Monitor and consider blocking repeated malicious IP addresses. 
	• Review server behavior for excessive HTTP 302 responses to suspicious inputs.
